---
title: "Getting started"
description: "The first hour with Taliesin, in steps that each end in something you can open in a browser."
---

This chapter takes you from nothing to a published page: install the tool, set up
a kernel for code, write your first document, and put it online.

## Install

Every tagged release attaches a prebuilt `.tar.gz` (with a `.sha256` beside it) for
Linux x86-64, macOS Apple silicon and macOS Intel. Unpack it, put the `taliesin`
binary on your `PATH`, and skip the rest of this section. Windows is not supported.

Otherwise it builds from source with a Rust toolchain. From the repo:

```sh
cargo build --release
```

That compiles 268 crates in roughly two minutes on four cores and peaks around
2.6 GB of memory; the result is one ~40 MB binary with KaTeX, its fonts, the
syntax definitions and every bundled script inside it, so nothing is fetched at
runtime.

The binary is `target/release/taliesin`. Put it on your `PATH` (a symlink, or a
small wrapper script that rebuilds on source changes before running it) so you
can just say `taliesin preview …` from any directory. Every command below works
either way: substitute `cargo run -p taliesin-server --` for `taliesin` if you'd
rather not install it on `PATH`.

## Set up a kernel (for code execution)

Prose, math, diagrams, and `{js}` cells need no setup. To *execute*
`{python}` / `{r}` cells you point the server at a Jupyter kernel. A one-time
Python setup:

```sh
python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate
pip install ipykernel numpy matplotlib   # + whatever your cells import
export TALIESIN_PYTHON="$(pwd)/.venv/bin/python"
```

For R cells, run `install.packages("IRkernel")` (then `IRkernel::installspec()`)
in your R and point `TALIESIN_R` at that R binary:

```sh
export TALIESIN_R="$(which R)"          # or a full path, e.g. /usr/local/bin/R
```

`{python}` and `{r}` cells each run against their own warm kernel, so a document
can mix both. Without a kernel, cells render as highlighted source and the preview
shows a quiet "kernel unavailable" note instead of failing, so you can always start
writing and add a kernel later. The kernel setup is covered in more depth (and with
the same callout) in [Executable content](code.tmd).

## Environment variables

Every knob the server reads from the environment, in one place. None are required:
the defaults work, and the only ones most projects ever set are the kernel paths.

| Variable | Default | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `TALIESIN_PYTHON` | `python3` | Python binary (with `ipykernel`) used for `{python}` cells |
| `TALIESIN_R` | `R` | R binary (with `IRkernel`) used for `{r}` cells |
| `TALIESIN_CELL_TIMEOUT` | `120` | Per-cell wall-clock seconds before a runaway cell is interrupted (SIGINT); `0` disables the cap |
| `TALIESIN_NO_CACHE` | unset | When set, skip the `_freeze/` execution cache (no reads, no writes) for this run |
| `TALIESIN_NO_EXEC` | unset | When set, never run code cells (render them as source); same as `--no-exec` |
| `TALIESIN_OPEN` | unset | When set, open the preview in a browser on start; same as `--open` |
| `TALIESIN_HOST` | unset | When set, expose the preview on the LAN; same as `--host` |
| `TALIESIN_NO_CLEAR` | unset | When set, keep the dev-server console from clearing the screen between renders |
| `TALIESIN_MERMAID_URL` | the vendored copy | Point the Mermaid loader at a different copy of the library (a relative path next to the site, or an absolute one). Diagrams are already offline without it |

`TALIESIN_*` flags that have a `--flag` equivalent (`--open`, `--host`, `--no-exec`)
are read on the same run; the flag and the variable mean the same thing, so use
whichever fits (the variable is handy in CI). The kernel paths and
`TALIESIN_CELL_TIMEOUT` come up most; the rest are situational.

## Scaffold a project (the fast path)

The quickest way to a previewable project is `init`, which scaffolds one into a
directory (the current one by default):

```sh
taliesin init my-site        # see below for what lands in it
taliesin preview my-site     # live, immediately
```

You get four things: a minimal `_site.yml`, a hello-world `index.tmd`, an `AGENTS.md`
briefing for coding agents, and a `.taliesin/` directory holding the JSON schemas your
editor uses to complete `_site.yml` and front-matter keys. `--template site` adds an
`about.tmd`; `--template book` adds the chapter files.

`init` refuses to overwrite: if any of them already exists it stops without touching
anything, so re-running it never clobbers your work. When you'd rather start from a
single document, skip straight to the next section.

## Your first document

Create `hello.tmd`:

````markdown
---
title: "Hello, Taliesin"
toc: true
---

# Introduction

A paragraph with **markdown**, inline math $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$, and a diagram:

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  write --> preview --> publish
```

# Compute

```{python}
import numpy as np
np.random.default_rng(0).normal(size=5).round(2)
```
````

Preview it live:

```sh
taliesin preview hello.tmd
```

Open `http://127.0.0.1:4321`. Edit the file and watch only the changed block
update in place. Ctrl-click (Cmd-click on Mac) any block to jump to its source
line in your editor.

## Build for publishing

When you're happy, `build` produces output (executing code cells against the
kernel, so the HTML carries the computed figures):

```sh
taliesin build hello.tmd                 # -> hello.html (one self-contained file)
taliesin build hello.tmd --out dist      # -> dist/index.html + copied local assets
```

Point `build`/`preview` at a **directory** instead and you get a whole
multi-page site or book (see [Slides, books & sites](formats.tmd)):

```sh
taliesin build blog/                     # -> blog/_site/   (one .html per page)
taliesin build docs/                     # -> docs/_book/   (a book, if _site.yml says so)
```

### Where the build goes

| You build | Taliesin writes |
|---|---|
| `build doc.tmd` | `doc.html` next to the source: one self-contained file |
| `build doc.tmd page.html` | `page.html` (your chosen path) instead of `<stem>.html` |
| `build doc.tmd --out dist` | `dist/index.html` plus the document's referenced local assets, a portable folder |
| `build site/` | `site/_site/` (a website) or `site/_book/` (a book, when `_site.yml` declares `chapters:`) |
| `build site/ --out public` | the same site, into `public/` instead of the default |

A site/book build (and the `--out` form) copies the local files your pages
*reference* (images, audio, videos, vendored scripts) next to the HTML, so the
output folder is self-contained and you can copy or `rsync` it as-is. The walker
**skips** what isn't deliverable: `.tmd` sources, `.bib`, `.Rproj`, anything whose
name starts with `_` or `.` (so `_freeze/` and dotfiles stay out), `*_cache` /
`*_files` working directories, and the output directory itself. A built-in
`404.html` is emitted unless you supply your own `404.tmd`, and a `mounts:` entry
is preview-only (the static build logs a warning with the command to build it
separately). The full set of flags is in the [CLI reference](../reference/cli.tmd);
common build-and-deploy patterns live in [Recipes](recipes.tmd).

## Publish it

The build output is **plain static HTML + assets** with no server runtime, so it
deploys anywhere that serves files.

- **GitHub Pages.** Build into `docs/` (or push `_site/`) on your default branch
  and enable Pages, or use an action that runs `taliesin build` and uploads the
  output directory as the Pages artifact.
- **Netlify / Vercel / Cloudflare Pages.** Set the build command to
  `cargo run -p taliesin-server -- build <dir>` and the publish directory to the
  emitted `_site/` / `_book/`.
- **Any static host / `rsync`.** Copy the output folder as-is. The `--out <dir>`
  form and the site/book builds copy referenced local assets next to the HTML, so
  the folder is self-contained.

### Deploy with GitHub Actions

A copy-paste workflow that builds on every push and publishes to GitHub Pages.
Enable **Settings → Pages → Source: GitHub Actions** once, then drop this in
`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`:

```yaml
name: Deploy
on:
  push: { branches: [main] }
permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write
concurrency: { group: pages, cancel-in-progress: true }

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: dtolnay/rust-toolchain@stable
      - uses: Swatinem/rust-cache@v2
      - name: Install Taliesin
        # point at wherever Taliesin lives; add --tag vX.Y for reproducible builds
        run: cargo install --git https://github.com/AJBogo9/taliesin --locked taliesin-server
      - name: Build the site
        run: taliesin build .          # website -> _site/   (a book emits _book/)
      - uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3
        with: { path: _site }

  deploy:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment:
      name: github-pages
      url: ${{ steps.deploy.outputs.page_url }}
    steps:
      - id: deploy
        uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4
```

`build` **executes code cells**, so if your pages run `{python}` / `{r}`, add a
step to install that interpreter plus `ipykernel` / `IRkernel` and set
`TALIESIN_PYTHON` / `TALIESIN_R`, otherwise those cells render as source in CI.

::: {.callout-note}
The interactive `{js}`/Three.js demos need to be served over `http(s)` (not opened
from `file://`): a cell's relative `import()` and Three.js's module are blocked on
`file://`. (d3 and Plot are bundled, so they need no network.) Once deployed (or
under `preview`) they are fully live.
:::
